    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable",
    "problem": "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$, and is differentiable at $x=-1$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nI. It is differentiable at $x=0$\n\nII. It  is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. It is continuous at $x=-1$",
    "worked_solution": "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity.",
